<!--Post: 5214--><!--Time: 1106951924-->
<!--p:--><tr bgcolor="#f7f7f7"><td valign=top width=200><a name="POST5214"><font face="Verdana" size="1"><b><!--email--><!--/email--><!--name-->George<!--/name--></b></a><br>Username: <!--field:uname-->George<!--/field--><br><br>Registered: <!--field:registered-->7-2004<!--/field--><br><!--field:ip_address--><!--/field--></font></font></td><td valign=top><font face="Verdana" size="1"><table width=100% border=0 cellspacing=0 cellpadding=0><tr><td align=left><font face="Verdana" size="1">Posted on Friday, January 28, 2005 - 2:38 pm: &nbsp;&nbsp;</font></td><td align=right></td></tr></table><hr noshade size=0><!--Text-->As movies are fantasy, and certain genres such as Sci-Fi and Horror can be extreme fantasy, might there be any mainstream movies which BTK enjoyed and which could be studied for images, dialogue, etc.? I started a thread for &#34;In Cold Blood&#34; several days ago. My idea here is to keep movies on one thread. BTK may well prefer hardcore porn the most but that doesn&#39;t mean he wouldn&#39;t enjoy any mainstream films. I have a few titles in mind, <BR>and will try to connect something between the movies and known BTK activity. Some of these are old but of course BTK could have rented. <BR><b>PSYCHO</b> 1960: BTK asked for this &#34;name&#34; specifically in one letter. <BR><b>SE7EN</b> 1995 : The serial murderer here, like BTK, controls everything, including the outcome. <BR><b>M</b> 1930: Very old but killer Peter Lorre speaks of something inside him which makes him kill, as does BTK. I don&#39;t buy it, but there it is. <BR><b>Follow Me Quietly</b> 1949: 59 minute movie about a serial killer called &#34;The Judge,&#34; who dispatches <BR>those he deems inferior. Fascinating and the case is solved by, believe it or not, what can only be called profiling. Like BTK, &#34;The Judge&#34; talks about motive. <BR><b>Silence of the Lambs</b> 1991: For obvious reasons. <BR><b>Shadow Of A Doubt</b> 1943: Hitchcock film about &#34;The Merry Widow Murderer,&#34; a man who <b>hates</b> and I mean <b>hates</b> women. &#34;..husbands who make a fortune and then leave it to their wives, their silly wives. You see them [women] in the best hotels every day by the thousands; eating the money, drinking the money, losing the money at bridge. Proud of their jewelry but nothing else. Horrible! Faded, fat, greedy women.&#34; --[Different character, addressing this comment] &#34;But they&#39;re alive, they&#39;re human beings!&#34; -- &#34;Are they? Are they human or are they fat, wheezing animals, hmm? And what happens to animals when they get too old and too fat?&#34; BTK didn&#39;t like women, as we know and as is profiled. The above lines were written by Thornton Wilder and this is one of the films pointed to when people say &#40;correctly, in my opinion&#41; that Hitchcock was a sadistic misogynist. <BR><b>White Heat</b> 1949: Mama&#39;s boy James Cagney is a sociopath who kills many unnecessarily, famous last line of film: &#39;Look Ma, I&#39;m on top of the world!&#39; then the large building Cagney is standing on explodes. The mother/son relationship in this film is most interesting, many have suggested BTK was similar to Cagney in this film. <BR><b>The Bad Seed</b> 1957: Possibly BTK&#39;s inspiration for his Factor X BS. <BR><b>Cape Fear</b> 1963: Original version. Robert Mitchum murders, rapes and terrorizes. I had a four second sound clip which sounds to me what BTK <BR>would say, but it&#39;s not supported. Mitchum also talks about and ogles menacingly other females in this film. <BR><b>Frenzy</b> 1972: Hitchcock film about a man who murders women via strangulation by necktie. <BR>I&#39;ll leave it at that. I&#39;m only looking for a chance bit of dialogue, or maybe a scene which might remind someone of BTK in some fashion. A direct quote is the ideal but people remember little visual quirks too and those little quirks stick in the memory. I cold have listed a couple of hundred films but didn&#39;t to save space, and I hope maybe one of the above &#40;a couple are hard to find, I know&#41; may summon a memory which relates to BTK.<!--/Text--></td></tr>
<!--/Post: 5214-->
<!--Post: 5578--><!--Time: 1107202997-->
<!--p:--><tr bgcolor="#cccccc"><td valign=top width=200><a name="POST5578"><font face="Verdana" size="1"><b><!--email--><!--/email--><!--name-->Featherw<!--/name--></b></a><br>Username: <!--field:uname-->Featherw<!--/field--><br><br>Registered: <!--field:registered-->1-2005<!--/field--><br><!--field:ip_address--><!--/field--></font></font></td><td valign=top><font face="Verdana" size="1"><table width=100% border=0 cellspacing=0 cellpadding=0><tr><td align=left><font face="Verdana" size="1">Posted on Monday, January 31, 2005 - 12:23 pm: &nbsp;&nbsp;</font></td><td align=right></td></tr></table><hr noshade size=0><!--Text-->Good job George...good list. <BR> <BR>As I was reading your post it occurred to me just how YOUNG or new psycho flicks were.  Already there were some Sci-fi&#39;s and dramas...but in the late 50&#39;s psycho thrillers were really almost unheard of.  The movie Psycho..one of the first ones.   <BR> <BR>Take a look at your list and you can see that psycho thrillers or movies with seriously demented characters were pretty scarce in the early, late 60&#39;s.  Now look...we have CSI, and a number of shows that present DEMENTED characters. <BR> <BR>You almost have to wonder if maybe BTK does not want to be a kind of MENTOR for these shows or shows of what he saw as the future back then......something on which all psycho thrillers will be based in the future.  If you really think about it, in some ways, that is exactly what has happened since the 70&#39;s.  The shows and movies that depict demented characters contain characters who make strange phone calls, or kill for a reason uknown and then position bodies.  TAUNTING the cops is also a major scene in some psycho thriller movies now.  I sometimes think the ZODIAC had a lot to do with the content and characters of some of these movies.  The ZODIAC inspired some of these things I am sure...and BTK wants his drama too...maybe? <BR> <BR> I keep wondering if ZODIAC was BTK&#39;s mentor and now BTK wants to be one..he really wants to have an AFFECT on something...that is for sure.   <BR>Thanks <BR> <BR> <BR><!--/Text--></td></tr>
<!--/Post: 5578-->
<!--Post: 5579--><!--Time: 1107203485-->
<!--p:--><tr bgcolor="#f7f7f7"><td valign=top width=200><a name="POST5579"><font face="Verdana" size="1"><b><!--email--><!--/email--><!--name-->Featherw<!--/name--></b></a><br>Username: <!--field:uname-->Featherw<!--/field--><br><br>Registered: <!--field:registered-->1-2005<!--/field--><br><!--field:ip_address--><!--/field--></font></font></td><td valign=top><font face="Verdana" size="1"><table width=100% border=0 cellspacing=0 cellpadding=0><tr><td align=left><font face="Verdana" size="1">Posted on Monday, January 31, 2005 - 12:31 pm: &nbsp;&nbsp;</font></td><td align=right></td></tr></table><hr noshade size=0><!--Text--><a href="http://www.sfheart.com/sixties/Films_60s_70s.html" target=_top>http://www.sfheart.com/sixties/Films_60s_70s.html</a> <BR>Popular Films &#39;60&#39;s and &#39;70&#39;s VHS/DVD <BR>-------- <BR>50&#39;s and 60&#39;s Horror Movies <BR><a href="http://www.mshepley.btinternet.co.uk/a50.htm" target=_top>http://www.mshepley.btinternet.co.uk/a50.htm</a> <BR>------- <BR>If you take a look at what some of these movies are about, you see that they are not near as AFFECTIVE as ones that we see today...in today&#39;s scary movies that depict demented characters, seems like the movies get more into the pshychology of the person who is affecting everyone with fear....adding mystery to the movie...I think BTK probably enjoys people getting into his psychology..because noone knows how he is...and he gets pleasure from that.  JMO.. <BR><!--/Text--></td></tr>
