<!--Post: 5628--><!--Time: 1107221853-->
<!--p:--><tr bgcolor="#f7f7f7"><td valign=top width=200><a name="POST5628"><font face="Verdana" size="1"><b><!--email--><!--/email--><!--name-->George<!--/name--></b></a><br>Username: <!--field:uname-->George<!--/field--><br><br>Registered: <!--field:registered-->7-2004<!--/field--><br><!--field:ip_address--><!--/field--></font></font></td><td valign=top><font face="Verdana" size="1"><table width=100% border=0 cellspacing=0 cellpadding=0><tr><td align=left><font face="Verdana" size="1">Posted on Monday, January 31, 2005 - 5:37 pm: &nbsp;&nbsp;</font></td><td align=right></td></tr></table><hr noshade size=0><!--Text-->Featherw: I have a sound wav file from the original &#34;Cape Fear.&#34; It won&#39;t play on this board, so I&#39;ll transcribe it. It won&#39;t have any punch if you haven&#39;t seen the thing, but the inflection Robert Mitchum uses in this line is done with such measured, wicked conviction as to be scary. I&#39;ll boldface the relevant portion: <BR> <BR>&#34;You just put the law in my hands <b>and I&#39;m.gonna..break..your..HEART WITH IT!&#34;</b> <BR> <BR>I agree with you about BTK&#39;s enjoying everyones speculation about him. If it stopped, I think he&#39;d slow down the communications. He has an audience these days.<!--/Text--></td></tr>
