<!--Post: 5695--><!--Time: 1107290978-->
<!--p:--><tr bgcolor="#f7f7f7"><td valign=top width=200><a name="POST5695"><font face="Verdana" size="1"><b><!--email--><!--/email--><!--name-->Lakesguy<!--/name--></b></a><br>Username: <!--field:uname-->Lakesguy<!--/field--><br><br>Registered: <!--field:registered-->1-2005<!--/field--><br><!--field:ip_address--><!--/field--></font></font></td><td valign=top><font face="Verdana" size="1"><table width=100% border=0 cellspacing=0 cellpadding=0><tr><td align=left><font face="Verdana" size="1">Posted on Tuesday, February 01, 2005 - 12:49 pm: &nbsp;&nbsp;</font></td><td align=right></td></tr></table><hr noshade size=0><!--Text-->How about the 1960 British movie &#39;Peeping Tom&#39;, about a young man who murders women and films it as it happens. This was shown at a lot of college campuses during the late &#39;60&#39;s and early &#39;70&#39;s, and became a &#39;cult classic.&#39;   <BR> <BR>The killer had an abusive father, a psychologist who subjected the son to horrible experiments. <BR> <BR>The character of the killer is interesting, because he is capable of genuine feeling for other people. In other words, he&#39;s not the stereotypical psycho killer.   <BR> <BR>You can read some interesting comments and some more about the film at imdb.com or amazon.com. <!--/Text--></td></tr>
<!--/Post: 5695-->
<!--Post: 5704--><!--Time: 1107296987-->
<!--p:--><tr bgcolor="#cccccc"><td valign=top width=200><a name="POST5704"><font face="Verdana" size="1"><b><!--email--><!--/email--><!--name-->Johno<!--/name--></b></a><br>Username: <!--field:uname-->Johno<!--/field--><br><br>Registered: <!--field:registered-->12-2004<!--/field--><br><!--field:ip_address--><!--/field--></font></font></td><td valign=top><font face="Verdana" size="1"><table width=100% border=0 cellspacing=0 cellpadding=0><tr><td align=left><font face="Verdana" size="1">Posted on Tuesday, February 01, 2005 - 2:29 pm: &nbsp;&nbsp;</font></td><td align=right></td></tr></table><hr noshade size=0><!--Text-->Rod Steiger as theater manager/serial killer Christopher Gill in 1968&#39;s &#39;No Way To Treat A Lady.&#39;<!--/Text--></td></tr>
<!--/Post: 5704-->
<!--Post: 5706--><!--Time: 1107297527-->
<!--p:--><tr bgcolor="#f7f7f7"><td valign=top width=200><a name="POST5706"><font face="Verdana" size="1"><b><!--email--><!--/email--><!--name-->Lubbock<!--/name--></b></a><br>Username: <!--field:uname-->Lubbock<!--/field--><br><br>Registered: <!--field:registered-->12-2004<!--/field--><br><!--field:ip_address--><!--/field--></font></font></td><td valign=top><font face="Verdana" size="1"><table width=100% border=0 cellspacing=0 cellpadding=0><tr><td align=left><font face="Verdana" size="1">Posted on Tuesday, February 01, 2005 - 2:38 pm: &nbsp;&nbsp;</font></td><td align=right></td></tr></table><hr noshade size=0><!--Text-->I&#39;m not sure when it came out but the original &#40;I believe&#41; version of Red Dragon - which contained the debut of the character Hannibal Lecter - may have been a favorite of BTK.  <BR> <BR>In it the psychopathic killer is involved in developing film.  <BR> <BR>He watched one family from a hiding place up in a tree at the perimeter of the victims&#39; property. <BR> <BR>I recommend the book over the movie - partially because to me Anthony Hopkins is the definitive Lecter &#40;sorry, I know this isn&#39;t a forum for movie reviews&#41; - I&#39;d like to check it out and read it again, and if I could I&#39;d do so from the main Wichita library, no telling who&#39;s hands the book might&#39;ve been in.<!--/Text--></td></tr>
<!--/Post: 5706-->
<!--Post: 5725--><!--Time: 1107306415-->
<!--p:--><tr bgcolor="#cccccc"><td valign=top width=200><a name="POST5725"><font face="Verdana" size="1"><b><!--email--><!--/email--><!--name-->Dee<!--/name--></b></a><br>Username: <!--field:uname-->Dee<!--/field--><br><br>Registered: <!--field:registered-->12-2004<!--/field--><br><!--field:ip_address--><!--/field--></font></font></td><td valign=top><font face="Verdana" size="1"><table width=100% border=0 cellspacing=0 cellpadding=0><tr><td align=left><font face="Verdana" size="1">Posted on Tuesday, February 01, 2005 - 5:06 pm: &nbsp;&nbsp;</font></td><td align=right></td></tr></table><hr noshade size=0><!--Text-->oK... and an avid lover of film would want to watch it on noThing less than a 42 in. Rear Projection HDTV-Ready Television; purchased for the Bargain price of $2,600.00.<!--/Text--></td></tr>
<!--/Post: 5725-->
<!--Post: 5753--><!--Time: 1107327287-->
<!--p:--><tr bgcolor="#f7f7f7"><td valign=top width=200><a name="POST5753"><font face="Verdana" size="1"><b><!--email--><!--/email--><!--name-->George<!--/name--></b></a><br>Username: <!--field:uname-->George<!--/field--><br><br>Registered: <!--field:registered-->7-2004<!--/field--><br><!--field:ip_address--><!--/field--></font></font></td><td valign=top><font face="Verdana" size="1"><table width=100% border=0 cellspacing=0 cellpadding=0><tr><td align=left><font face="Verdana" size="1">Posted on Tuesday, February 01, 2005 - 10:54 pm: &nbsp;&nbsp;</font></td><td align=right></td></tr></table><hr noshade size=0><!--Text-->Tippy, you did pick the most disturbing film of the lot. I agree with Leonard Maltin: three stars, <BR>but &#34;extremely disconcerting.&#34; I didn&#39;t have any problem with it, as it&#39;s a fictional movie, but I view film differently than most. I&#39;m more interested in the &#34;behind the camera&#34; stuff, especially the screenplay and the direction. I love the scene when Morgan Freeman is in the library photocopying old books; Milton, Dante, Aquinas, etc. The ending is predictable but I like the overall premise. BTK probably loved it.<!--/Text--></td></tr>
